  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Immunizations are essential, safe, and effective methods to build immunity against infectious diseases by mimicking natural infection without causing the disease itself. They prevent serious illnesses—such as measles, polio, and pertussis—reduce health risks, and are critical for protecting vulnerable individuals through community immunity. How Immunizations WorkVaccines stimulate the body’s immune system to recognize and fight pathogens (viruses or bacteria) without causing the disease. If the person is exposed to the disease later, their immune system is prepared to destroy it quickly. Types of Immunization and Key Vaccines Childhood Series: Protects against diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis (DTaP), measles, mumps, rubella (MMR), polio, and varicella (chickenpox). Adolescent/Adult Boosters: Includes tetanus/diphtheria (Tdap), HPV, and meningitis vaccines. Annual Vaccines: Influenza (flu) and COVID-19 vaccines. Specialized Vaccines: RSV and pneumococcal vaccines for adults, particularly older individuals. Immunization Benefits Disease Prevention: Significantly lowers the risk of contracting severe, life-threatening infections. Community Immunity (Herd Immunity): High vaccination rates prevent disease spread, protecting those who cannot be vaccinated. Long-term Health: Reduces the likelihood of long-term health problems caused by infections. Side Effects of ImmunizationVaccines are monitored closely and are generally safe. Side effects are usually mild and temporary, including: Pain, redness, or swelling at the injection site. Low-grade fever or headache. For the most accurate, up-to-date schedule, consult the recommendations from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention | CDC (.gov).",
